WEBVTT

00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:02.617
Hi, I'm Parsley!

00:00:02.617 --> 00:00:07.926
When camera poses drift, feed-forward Gaussian splatting fails quietly: input views look

00:00:07.926 --> 00:00:09.696
fine, new views don't.

00:00:09.696 --> 00:00:11.908
Here's how VoxelTTO fixes that.

00:00:13.225 --> 00:00:14.001
Two ideas.

00:00:14.001 --> 00:00:16.719
Anchor the Gaussians to voxels, not pixels.

00:00:16.719 --> 00:00:21.378
Then, for each scene, fit small adapters until the frozen backbone's cameras

00:00:21.378 --> 00:00:22.931
agree with your poses.

00:00:24.275 --> 00:00:27.442
Most feed-forward models make one Gaussian per pixel.

00:00:27.442 --> 00:00:32.984
Neighbouring views see the same surface, so it gets overlapping Gaussians: floaters and shells.

00:00:33.404 --> 00:00:38.192
VoxelTTO fuses the backbone's tokens into one global voxel grid, encodes it

00:00:38.192 --> 00:00:42.581
with a sparse 3D U-Net, and splits each voxel into Gaussians.

00:00:43.958 --> 00:00:48.351
Images climb the frozen backbone, which also decodes a camera for every view.

00:00:48.771 --> 00:00:53.307
Adapters go on the query, key and value projections of layers thirteen to forty.

00:00:53.307 --> 00:00:55.251
Layers one to twelve get nothing.

00:00:55.671 --> 00:00:57.162
The loss isn't photometric.

00:00:57.162 --> 00:01:00.889
It compares the decoded cameras with the poses you supplied.

00:01:01.309 --> 00:01:05.070
Twenty steps bend the backbone's attention until its cameras agree.

00:01:05.070 --> 00:01:07.702
The Gaussians move because the features move.

00:01:09.175 --> 00:01:12.979
On Tanks and Temples, rotation error falls from one point six three

00:01:12.979 --> 00:01:15.198
six degrees to point two six two.

00:01:15.198 --> 00:01:18.685
The view gap shrinks from nine point oh three decibels to

00:01:18.685 --> 00:01:19.952
one point six six.

00:01:21.308 --> 00:01:23.693
So the fit doesn't make the model better.

00:01:23.693 --> 00:01:26.673
It decides whose coordinate frame wins, and always picks yours.

00:01:26.673 --> 00:01:29.952
If your poses are wrong, it drags the scene onto them.

00:01:31.775 --> 00:01:36.102
To recap: voxels instead of pixels, adapters fitted to your poses, and

00:01:36.102 --> 00:01:38.987
a trade worth checking against your own cameras.

00:01:39.407 --> 00:01:41.559
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:41.559 --> 00:01:42.174
I'm Parsley.

00:01:42.174 --> 00:01:42.482
Bye!
